sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1 (task_vms is record-only), R2, R4 (apps.path is Node work), decision 8 (one node:update). docs/reference/node-settings.md.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: node:update
  %% kind: node.update
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: node:update 12<br/>--apps-path=/srv/apps
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:update 12 --apps-path=/srv/apps`
  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--name=NAME] [--platform=PLATFORM] [--apps-path=PATH] [--task-vms=JSON] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node, by id or exact name
  %%> - at least one of: a new name, a platform, the apps folder, or the task VM host settings as a JSON object
  %%> - an empty value unsets a setting: `--apps-path=` sends a null path, `--task-vms=null` removes the task VM host settings
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, lock the Node<br/>task #5131 · Node 12
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12
  %%> - an apps folder needs an active, managed Linux Node
  %%> - no other lifecycle change runs for Node 12; the Gateway holds the Node's name lock, and the new name's lock for a rename
  %%> Checks for the name:
  %%> - ASCII letters, digits, dashes and underscores, at most 63 characters, unique among Nodes
  %%> Checks for the platform:
  %%> - `linux`, `macos` or `ipados`, and only on an unmanaged Node: no pinned SSH host key and no role
  %%> Checks for the apps folder:
  %%> - absolute and normalized: not `/`, no trailing or repeated `/`, no `.` or `..` part
  %%> - not the managed user's home (`/home/<user>`, as Orbit creates it) or an ancestor of it, and not inside a hidden folder of that home
  %%> - not overlapping `/boot`, `/dev`, `/etc`, `/proc`, `/run`, `/sys`, `/usr`, `/opt/orbit`, `/var/lib/orbit`, `/var/www`, or the Gateway checkout and release layout
  %%> - not equal to, or inside, an existing Instance checkout on Node 12
  %%> Checks for `task_vms`:
  %%> - typed fields only, such as `cidr`, `max_vms`, `pool`, `image_alias`
  %%> - the bridge `cidr` overlaps no other task VM host
  %%> Decides:
  %%> - whether the change touches Node 12, or only the record
  alt only record fields change
    Note over GW: Store the record<br/>R1: no run · decision 8
    %%> When:
    %%> - the request changes only the name, the platform or `task_vms`
    %%> - or it unsets the apps folder on a Node without an active `app-dev` role, so no folder is needed
    %%> Why no run:
    %%> - nothing on Node 12 changes now; the name and platform are registry facts, and `task_vms` applies when the next task VM is created on Node 12
    %%> - task VMs that already run keep their settings
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12's record is saved
    %%> - no Swamp run starts (R1)
    GW-->>CLI: Node updated<br/>Node 12
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the Node with its new values, at once; there is nothing to follow
  else apps path changes
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-update
    %%> When:
    %%> - the request changes the apps folder, alone or together with other fields
    %%> - a new name or platform in the same request is saved at once; it needs no run
    %%> - unsetting it on a Node with an active `app-dev` role prepares the default folder, `<managed-user-home>/apps`
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the new apps folder, and the managed user and group that must own it
    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
    %%> Not stored yet:
    %%> - the settings stay unchanged until the run succeeds (R2)
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5131
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task number, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    SW->>N: Create the apps folder<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/directory apply
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - creates `/srv/apps` and any missing folders above it
    %%> - gives `/srv/apps` the managed user and group and mode `0755`
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/directory` behavior and sudo
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - existing Instances don't move; their checkouts stay where they are
    %%> - new development Instances use `/srv/apps/<project>/<instance>`
    SW->>N: Check owner and mode<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · stat
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the folder's type, owner, group and mode in one `stat`
    %%> Why:
    %%> - it is the one end check: the Gateway compares its output with the intent, a real folder of the managed user and group, mode 0755
    alt folder is ready
      N-->>SW: orbit:orbit · 0755
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the check passed
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
      %%> How the Gateway learns it:
      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
      Note over GW: Save the settings<br/>apps.path set · task succeeded
      %%> Result:
      %%> - Node 12's apps folder is `/srv/apps`; any `task_vms` change in the same request is saved with it
      %%> - task #5131 succeeds
    else a step fails
      N--xSW: wrong owner or exit ≠ 0
      %%> When:
      %%> - the folder can't be created, a file is in the way, or the check doesn't match
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      %%> The Gateway reads:
      %%> - which step failed and its error code
      Note over GW: Keep the old settings<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix
      %%> Result:
      %%> - Node 12's settings stay unchanged
      %%> - task #5131 fails
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - folders created before the failure stay; there is no rollback
      %%> - that is safe: an empty folder is harmless, and a rerun leaves it as it is
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5131
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
    end
  end
